<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edinburgh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edinburgh is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, on the southern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a>. Its castle rock was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/din-eidyn">Din Eidyn</a>, the stronghold of the British kingdom of Gododdin, until the Northumbrians took it, probably in 638. From the 15th century it was Scotland's capital, the seat of the royal court and parliament, and in the 18th century it was a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-enlightenment">Scottish Enlightenment</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edinburgh.jpg" alt="Edinburgh" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Colin · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Edinburgh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Edinburgh lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s Central Belt on the southern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a>, between the firth to the north and the Pentland Hills to the south. The modern city is often said to be built on seven hills, among them Calton Hill, Arthur's Seat and the Castle Rock, which gave rise to comparisons with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Castle Rock is a volcanic plug, and the crag and the hollows around it formed an ideal natural strongpoint on which Edinburgh Castle was built. The nickname Auld Reekie, Scots for Old Smoky, refers to the smoke over the Old Town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest known human habitation in the area was at Cramond, where a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> camp site dates to about 8500 BCE. The name comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/din-eidyn">Eidyn</a>, the name of the region in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbric">Cumbric</a>, a Brittonic Celtic language, centred on the stronghold of Din Eidyn, believed to have stood on Castle Rock. A siege of Din Eidyn by Oswald, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> of Northumbria, in 638 began three centuries of Germanic influence in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and the town was subsumed in 954 by the kingdom the English called Scotland. As Cumbric gave way to Northumbrian Old English and then Scots, din was replaced by burh, producing Edinburgh.</p><p>In 1603 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a> succeeded to the English throne, and in 1650 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>'s New Model Army occupied the city. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-union-1707">Acts of Union</a> of 1706 and 1707 united the two kingdoms as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> from 1 May 1707, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-scotland">Parliament of Scotland</a> merged into that of Great Britain at Westminster. Many Scots opposed the Union, and there were riots in the city.</p><p>The population rose from 49,000 in 1751 to 136,000 in 1831, mostly by migration from the countryside. Overcrowding in the Old Town's tenements and poor sanitation brought cholera outbreaks in 1832, 1848 and 1866. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> overtook Edinburgh as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s largest city by 1821. Improvements under Lord Provost William Chambers in the 1860s began to transform the Old Town, and major slum clearance followed in the 1960s and 1970s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Edinburgh is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and one of its 32 council areas, recognised as the capital since at least the 15th century. It is the seat of the Scottish Parliament and Government and of the highest courts in Scotland. The Palace of Holyroodhouse is the monarch's official residence in Scotland. The Old Town and the New Town together are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, and the University of Edinburgh was founded in 1582.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edinburgh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal burghs such as Berwick, Perth, Aberdeen and <b>Edinburgh</b> held monopolies of foreign trade, exporting wool, hides, fish and later coal and salt to the Low Countries, the Baltic and France; from the fifteenth century a Scottish staple port in the Netherlands, from 1541 at Veere, handled much…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Scottish Enlightenment flourished in <b>Edinburgh</b>, Glasgow and Aberdeen, the Italian Enlightenment in Milan and Naples, and in Prussia Kant published his Critique of Pure Reason in 1781.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Darwin studied medicine at <b>Edinburgh</b> from 1825, then learnt science as natural theology at Cambridge.</blockquote><a href="/work/on-the-origin-of-species">On the Origin of Species · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…Francis Brothers and Day of London published it as a work written and composed by James Yorkston of <b>Edinburgh</b>, with music arranged by Edmund Forman; that edition says it was reprinted with permission from Kohler and Son of Edinburgh, which implies an earlier Scottish edition, but no copy…</blockquote><a href="/songs/molly-malone">Molly Malone</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince Philip, Duke of <b>Edinburgh</b>, commissioned a setting from William Lovelady for his 75th birthday; an abridged version for four-part choir and organ was sung at his funeral in St George's Chapel, Windsor, on 17 April 2021.</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-104">Psalm 104</a></li><li><blockquote>One of the earliest printings of the music was published in <b>Edinburgh</b> in 1840 by Paterson as &quot;Bonnie Loch Loman&quot;, credited to &quot;a lady&quot;, with arrangements by Finlay Dunn and John Thomson.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-bonnie-banks-o-loch-lomond">The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond</a></li></ul></section>
